mmSupernaturals — provenance and licence notice
================================================

This repository is a community fork. It is not the original author's release, and it is
not endorsed by them. The README records how the lineage was established; this file
records what each layer of the code is licensed under, because that is not one answer.

1. 2011 — "Supernatural Players" (a Bukkit plugin) by Matterz.

   The project's repository (github.com/Matterz/SupernaturalPlayers), the account that
   held it, and its dev.bukkit.org page no longer exist. The one surviving copy of that
   tree (github.com/vayder69/SupernaturalPlayers) carries a single licence file,
   License-fdl.txt — the GNU Free Documentation License, version 1.3 — and no other.
   A copy is kept here at licenses/GNU-FDL-1.3.txt, unmodified.

   Copyright in that code stays with its author. Deleting a repository does not place a
   work in the public domain, and it does not revoke a licence already granted for copies
   obtained while the work was published.

2. 2011–2012 — a continuation of the plugin.

   That tree adds gpl-3.0.txt and declares GPL-3.0. Whether the author of the 2011 code
   authorised that relicensing is not known: the commit that adds the file is titled
   "Everything is back!" and says nothing about it, and both the repository and the
   account are gone.

3. This fork.

   It inherits that tree and keeps GPL-3.0 as its licence. The full text is at LICENSE - the
   same file as gpl-3.0.txt, under the name GitHub reads - so changes made in this
   repository are licensed GPL-3.0 by their authors.

4. What this repository does not carry.

   No Minecraft client or server jar, no server core (Paper, Paperclip, Spigot, Bukkit), no
   patched game jar, and no Mojang assets are committed here - not in the working tree, and
   not in any commit since 2011. The integration tests download a Paper server into
   target/it/server/paper.jar when they run, and target/ is ignored. The published jar
   contains this project's own classes and nothing else; paper-api is a `provided`
   dependency and is not bundled or shaded.

   One piece of third-party data used to be embedded, in test code only, and is not any
   more. Skins.java, under src/test/java/me/matterz/supernaturals/it/harness/, held eleven
   real accounts' signed skin textures, read from Mojang's profile and session APIs, so that
   the harness's stand-in players would wear the race they demonstrate. They were other
   people's skins, copied into a public repository to decorate a test server, and they have
   been removed. What replaced them is not another borrowed set: each race is now drawn from
   two flat colours and a trim stripe by SkinArt.java, which this project owns, and served to
   the client from a loopback port by SkinServer.java. Nothing is downloaded, nothing is
   signed, and harness-skins.properties - ignored, never committed - exists only to let a
   checkout put its own texture on instead.

   Those eleven values are still in this repository's history, in every commit before the one
   that removed them. Taking them out of a published history means rewriting every commit and
   every release tag, which this project will not do for tidiness alone. They were public
   profile data to begin with; if you are one of the eleven and want them gone, open an issue
   and they will be gone.

